::ENG3_U02_L02_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Passage: In a mountain village, a baker saved one loaf each day for travelers who arrived after sunset. Years later, when snow blocked the pass, those same travelers returned with carts of flour. Which universal theme is best supported? {
=A. Kindness offered to others can return as support in a time of need.#Correct. The baker's generosity later becomes help. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. Bakers should never share food with travelers.#This contradicts the positive outcome. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. Snow always helps communities survive.#The snow creates a problem, not help. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Travelers cannot be trusted.#The travelers return with help. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Passage: In a crowded apartment building, a teenager repaired neighbors' phones without charging them. When her own computer broke before an interview, the neighbors found parts, tools, and a quiet room where she could prepare. Which theme matches the passage? {
~A. Technology prevents people from cooperating.#Technology is part of the setting, but cooperation is positive. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. Generosity can build a community of support.#Correct. The teenager's help leads to the neighbors helping her. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. Interviews are impossible without neighbors.#This is too narrow and literal. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. People should avoid repairing phones.#The passage presents repair positively. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Text A: A baker shares loaves with travelers. Later, travelers bring flour during a snowstorm. Text B: A teenager repairs phones for neighbors. Later, neighbors help fix her computer. What shared theme do the texts develop? {
~A. Different jobs require different tools.#This is true on the surface but not the shared theme. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. Food is more useful than technology.#This compares details but misses the theme. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. Acts of service can create reciprocal care.#Correct. Both texts show service returning as care. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. Snowstorms and interviews are identical problems.#The problems differ. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] How do Text A and Text B develop the shared theme differently? Text A uses a mountain village and travelers; Text B uses an apartment building and technology repair. {
~A. Text A has no setting, while Text B has a setting.#Both texts have settings. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. Text A rejects generosity, while Text B supports it.#Both support generosity. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. Text A and Text B have identical plots.#The plots are similar in theme but not identical. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=D. Text A develops the theme through survival supplies, while Text B develops it through practical help and technology.#Correct. This explains different development across settings. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Which is a complete universal theme statement? {
~A. Memory#This is only a topic. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. A character remembers the past#This is a plot idea, not a theme. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. Remembering the past can help people choose a wiser future.#Correct. This is a complete insight about life. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. The story has memory in it#This is vague and incomplete. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q06::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Text A: An old sailor teaches children to read stars after ships begin using machines. Text B: A coding mentor teaches students to sketch ideas before opening laptops. What theme do both texts share? {
~A. New tools make old skills worthless.#Both texts argue the opposite. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. Foundational skills remain valuable even when tools change.#Correct. Both texts show older or basic skills still mattering. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. People should reject all technology.#Neither text rejects technology completely. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Stars and laptops have the same purpose.#This is a surface-level false comparison. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q07::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Which evidence from Text A supports the theme that foundational skills remain valuable? Text A: An old sailor teaches children to read stars after ships begin using machines. "Tools change," he said, "but people still need to know how to look up." {
~A. "old sailor"#This identifies a character but not the theme directly. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. "ships began using machines"#This gives context but not the full theme. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. "people still need to know how to look up"#Correct. This directly supports the value of foundational skill. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. "children"#This identifies learners but does not state the theme. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q08::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Which evidence from Text B supports the theme that thinking matters before using tools? Text B: A coding mentor taught students to sketch their ideas on paper before opening a laptop. "Screens are useful," she said, "but thinking begins before the screen turns on." {
~A. "coding mentor"#This identifies the speaker but not the theme. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. "thinking begins before the screen turns on"#Correct. This directly supports the theme about thinking before tools. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. "students"#This identifies people but not the theme. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. "laptop"#This names the tool, but the quote explains the theme. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q09::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Text A is set at sea, and Text B is set in a modern classroom. Why can they still share a universal theme? {
~A. Universal themes depend on identical settings.#This is false. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. Universal themes only happen in modern stories.#This is false. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. Universal themes can appear in different times and places because they address human concerns.#Correct. Setting can change while the human concern remains. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. Universal themes do not need evidence.#Theme analysis requires evidence. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q10::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Passage: A queen in an old tale gives away her crown to save a village. A mayor in a modern story gives up his office to expose the truth. Which theme do both plots support? {
~A. Leadership means keeping power at all costs.#Both characters give up power. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. Crowns and offices are the same object.#This is a surface comparison. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. Villages and modern towns cannot share themes.#They can share human concerns. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=D. True leadership may require sacrifice.#Correct. Both characters give up status for others or truth. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q11::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Which statement best tracks a theme across two texts? {
=A. Both texts explore courage, but the first shows physical courage while the second shows moral courage.#Correct. This names a shared theme and explains different development. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. Both texts have characters.#This is too general. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. The first text is old and the second text is new.#This names a difference but not a theme. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Both texts are exactly the same.#Theme comparison does not require identical texts. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q12::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Text A: A farmer plants seeds during a drought. Text B: A student starts a club after everyone says no one will join. Which universal theme best connects the texts? {
~A. Farming is the same as school.#This is a surface comparison. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. Hope often begins before success is visible.#Correct. Both characters act before results are certain. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. Droughts always improve schools.#This is unsupported. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Clubs are more important than crops.#This is not the shared theme. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q13::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Which is NOT a strong universal theme statement? {
~A. Responsibility can grow when one person acts first.#This is a complete theme. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. Fear can be faced through small acts of courage.#This is a complete theme. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. Friendship#Correct. Friendship is a topic, not a complete theme statement. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. Memory can guide people through change.#This is a complete theme. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q14::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Text A: A child in a folktale returns a lost coin. Text B: A teenager in a city returns a lost wallet. Which shared theme is best? {
=A. Honesty matters even when no one is watching.#Correct. Both actions show honesty across different settings. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. Cities are more honest than folktales.#This is unsupported. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. Lost objects always return by magic.#This is unsupported. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Coins and wallets are identical objects.#They are related but not identical. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q15::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] What is the main difference between a topic and a theme? {
=A. A topic names the subject, while a theme states an insight about that subject.#Correct. This explains the difference clearly. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. A topic requires evidence, but a theme does not.#Both can be discussed with evidence, and theme must be supported. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. A topic is always longer than a theme.#Length does not define the difference. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. A theme is only one word.#A theme should usually be a complete idea. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q16::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Text A: A village shares tools to repair a roof before winter. Text B: Neighbors share internet access so students can finish assignments. Which theme do both develop? {
=A. Communities become stronger when people share resources.#Correct. Both texts show shared resources solving problems. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. Winter is the same as school.#This is a surface comparison. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. Internet access is more important than shelter.#This is not the shared theme. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Tools are only physical objects.#Text B's support is not only physical tools. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q17::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Which response best compares theme development? {
~A. Text A and Text B both use words.#This is not meaningful theme analysis. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. Text A is about a storm, and Text B is about a failure, so they cannot share a theme.#Different events can share a theme. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. Text A is better because it has weather.#This is unsupported and not analytical. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=D. Text A develops resilience through survival after a storm, while Text B develops resilience through rebuilding after a public failure.#Correct. This compares how the same theme develops differently. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q18::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Text A: A young messenger keeps walking despite a mountain path. Text B: A nurse keeps working despite a crowded hospital. Which theme best connects them? {
~A. Mountains and hospitals are the same setting.#They are different settings. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. Perseverance means continuing when circumstances are difficult.#Correct. Both characters continue through difficulty. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. Messages are always medical.#This is unsupported. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Crowded places prevent all courage.#This is too absolute and unsupported. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q19::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Which evidence best supports a theme about belonging? Passage: The new student sat alone until the lunch table shifted, one chair at a time, leaving a space in the middle. {
~A. "new student"#This identifies a character but does not show belonging by itself. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. "sat alone"#This shows isolation, but not the movement toward belonging. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. "leaving a space in the middle"#Correct. This detail shows others making room for the student. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. "lunch table"#This gives setting, not theme by itself. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q20::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Which pair of texts is most likely to share a universal theme? {
~A. One text lists train schedules, and one text gives a recipe.#These are not literary theme-focused texts. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. One text defines a word, and one text labels a diagram.#These are unlikely to develop literary themes. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. One text gives weather data, and one text gives a phone number.#These do not develop literary themes. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=D. One text shows a soldier forgiving an enemy, and another shows a student forgiving a friend.#Correct. Both can develop a theme about forgiveness across different settings. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q21::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Text A: A character leaves home to protect family honor. Text B: A character speaks the truth even when it disappoints friends. Which shared theme is strongest? {
=A. Integrity may require difficult personal choices.#Correct. Both characters make hard choices based on values. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. Travel is always honorable.#Only Text A includes leaving home. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. Friendship always prevents truth.#Text B shows truth despite disappointment. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Families and friends are identical.#They are different relationships. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q22::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Which sentence best uses academic vocabulary for theme analysis? {
~A. The texts are about stuff people do.#This is too vague. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. Theme is when there are characters.#This misunderstands theme. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. The thing in both stories is good.#This is vague and informal. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=D. Both texts develop the universal theme that responsibility grows through action.#Correct. This uses academic vocabulary and states a complete theme. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q23::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Text A: An apprentice admits a mistake to a master craftsperson. Text B: A lab assistant reports an error before the experiment continues. Which theme do both texts develop? {
~A. Mistakes should always be hidden.#Both characters admit errors. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. Crafts and science are identical.#They are different fields. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. Honesty can protect the quality of shared work.#Correct. Both texts show honesty preventing greater harm. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. Apprentices and lab assistants never learn.#This is unsupported. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q24::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Which statement best avoids overgeneralizing a theme? {
~A. All people always help others when they can.#This is too absolute. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. No one can ever change without suffering.#This is too absolute. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. Every old story teaches the same lesson.#This is too broad. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=D. Some texts suggest that help offered freely can strengthen a community.#Correct. This states a theme without claiming it applies to every person or every text. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
}

::ENG3_U02_L02_Q25::[MLA.ENG3.R.02] Text A: A character lights a lamp for travelers in an old village. Text B: A character posts clear directions online during an emergency. Which comparison best tracks the shared theme? {
~A. Both texts prove lamps and websites are the same.#This is a surface comparison. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. Only old villages can show responsibility.#Text B also shows responsibility. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. Both texts show responsibility through guidance, but one uses a physical light and the other uses digital directions.#Correct. This tracks the shared theme and different development. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. Neither text includes a helpful action.#Both include helpful guidance. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}
